9176:             samples the perr_l signal asserted two cycles after a data transfer, it uses an
9177:             internal interrupt to report a parity error to the CPU (Section 6.2.13.2).
9178: 
9179: 6.2.8 I/O Space Cycles
9180: 
9181:             The IOC generates addresses in this region for transactions to the I/O region
9182:             specified by the PCI architecture. This region supports the same access types
9183:             and masked-address generation as sparse space (Section 6.2.6), with the
9184:             following exceptions:
9185: 
9186:             � I/O space is accessed with CPU address bits a<33:32> = 01 and
9187:                   a<31:29> = 110.
9188: 
9189:             � PCI address bits ad<31:27> = 00000 and the IOC_HAE register is not
9190:                   used.
9191: 
9192:             � PCI address bits ad<1:0> come directly from physical address bits a<6:5>
9193:                   during CPU-initiated PCI transfers to I/O space.
9194: 
9195:             � An I/O read or write command (Table 6-2) is issued on the c_be_l<3:0>
9196:                   pins coincident with a read or write operation to this region.
9197: 
9198:             The I/O region supports all masked and unmasked transfers that are supported
9199:             in sparse space.
9200: 
9201: 6.2.9 Configuration Cycles
9202: 
9203:             The PCI architecture specifies a configuration address space for defining
9204:             configuration registers that are used to initialize and configure devices.
9205:             The IOC supports transfers to the configuration address space to access
9206:             the configuration registers. This region supports the same access types and
9207:             masked address generation as sparse space (Section 6.2.6), with the following
9208:             exceptions:
9209: 
9210:             � Configuration space is accessed with CPU address bits a<33:32> = 01 and
9211:                   a<31:29> = 111.
9212: 
9213:             � PCI address bits ad<31:27> = 00000 and the IOC_HAE register is not
9214:                   used.
9215: 
9216:             � PCI address bits ad<1:0> come directly from configuration cycle type
9217:                   register (IOC_CONF, Section 6.4.2) bits <1:0> during CPU-initiated PCI
9218:                   transfers to configuration space.
9219: 
9220:                                                                                                                I/O Controller 6-15
9221:             � A configuration space read or write command (Table 6-2) is issued on the
9222:                   c_be_l<3:0> pins coincident with a read or write operation to this region.
9223: 
9224:             During configuration cycles, addresses are restricted both by the encoding
9225:             requirements of PCI configuration cycles (as described in the PCI Local Bus
9226:             Specification, Revision 2.0) and by the system-dependent implementation of
9227:             PCI initialization device select (IDSEL) encoding. Configuration cycles use one
9228:             cycle of address stepping; that is, the address and command are driven for two
9229:             consecutive cycles.
9230: 
9231: 6.2.10 Special Cycles and Interrupt Acknowledge Cycles
9232: 
9233:             The PCI special cycle command implements a simple broadcast mech-
9234:             anism that allows the broadcast of processor status information and
9235:             sideband signaling. The command is not addressed to a specific device,
9236:             and the receivers determine whether the message applies to them.
9237:             Section 6.3.11 describes two supported sideband signals. Also see the
9238:             PCI Local Bus Specification, Revision 2.0 for more information.
9239: 
9240: 6.2.10.1 Special Cycle
9241:             The CPU can initiate a special cycle transfer on the PCI by executing an STL
9242:             instruction to the interrupt vector and special cycle register (IOC_IACK_SC,
9243:             Section 6.4.13). When this register is written, the IOC initiates a PCI cycle
9244:             with a special cycle bus command. During a special cycle, the IOC generates
9245:             an unspecified address on the PCI that is ignored by the PCI devices. The
9246:             write data generated by the STL instruction passes unmodified through the
9247:             IOC to the PCI during the data phase of the special cycle. The special cycle
9248:             message is encoded in the write data as described in the PCI Local Bus
9249:             Specification, Revision 2.0.
9250: 
9251:             The special cycle address region is accessed only by STL instructions. This
9252:             region supports the same write access types and masked address generation as
9253:             sparse space (Section 6.2.6), with the following exceptions:
9254: 
9255:             � Write access to the IOC_IACK_SC register is enabled when CPU address
9256:                   bits a<33:32> = 01 and a<31:29> = 101.
9257: 
9258:             � The IOC_HAE register is used to generate PCI address bits ad<31:27>
9259:                   under all circumstances.
9260: 
9261:             � A special cycle command (Table 6-2) is issued on the c_be_l<3:0> pins, and
9262:                   a PCI interrupt acknowledge cycle follows as a result.
9263: 
9264: 6-16 I/O Controller
9265: 6.2.10.2 Interrupt Acknowledge Cycle
9266:             During a PCI interrupt acknowledge cycle, the IOC can acknowledge an
9267:             interrupt and receive an interrupt vector. This allows external interrupt-
9268:             arbitration hardware to supply interrupt vectors for PCI devices.
9269:             The CPU can initiate an interrupt acknowledge cycle by executing either an
9270:             LDL or an LDQ instruction, depending on the size of the returned interrupt
9271:             vector. Read access types and mask generation are identical to sparse space
9272:             (Section 6.2.6), with the following exceptions:
9273:             � Read access to the IOC_IACK_SC register is enabled when CPU address
9274:                   bits a<33:32> = 01 and a<31:29> = 101.
9275:             � The IOC_HAE register is used to generate PCI address bits ad<31:27>
9276:                   under all circumstances.
9277:             � An interrupt acknowledge cycle command (Table 6-2) is issued on the
9278:                   c_be_l<3:0> pins, and a PCI interrupt acknowledge cycle follows as a
9279:                   result.
9280: 
9281:                                                                  Note
9282:                   Digital recommends that software use only address 1A0000000
9283:                   to reference the IOC_IACK_SC register. However, in the current
9284:                   chip implementation, CPU address bits <28:0> are not decoded to
9285:                   access the IOC_IACK_SC register, and any address in the range
9286:                   1A0000000..1BFFFFFE0 will alias to this single register. For
9287:                   implementation convenience, the chip will drive the supplied address
9288:                   onto the PCI bus.
9289: 
9290:             When the IOC_IACK_SC register is read, the IOC initiates a PCI cycle with
9291:             an interrupt acknowledge bus command. External interrupt-arbitration logic
9292:             must supply an interrupt vector in response to the interrupt acknowledge bus
9293:             cycle. The interrupt vector data is returned to the CPU as the IOC_IACK_SC
9294:             register read data. During an interrupt acknowledge cycle, the IOC generates
9295:             an unspecified address on the PCI that is ignored by the PCI devices.
9296:             During CPU-initiated interrupt acknowledge cycles, PCI address bits ad<31:0>
9297:             generated by the IOC are unspecified.
9298: 
9567:             to system memory. A set of three IOC registers is associated with each PCI
9568:             target window: the window base; window mask; and translated base registers
9569:             (IOC_W_BASE<1:0>, Section 6.4.9; IOC_W_MASK<1:0>, Section 6.4.10; and
9570:             IOC_T_BASE<1:0>, Section 6.4.11).
9571: 
9572:             The window mask register IOC_W_MASK provides a mask corresponding
9573:             to bits <31:20> of an incoming PCI address. The size of each window is
9574:             programmed to be from 1 MB to 4 GB by masking bits of the incoming PCI
9575:             address with the IOC_W_MASK register.
9576: 
9577:             Table 6-6 shows the supported values of IOC_W_MASK register bits <31:20>;
9578:             other values are unsupported.
9579: 
9580:                                                    I/O Controller 6-23
9581: Table 6-6 PCI Window Mask
9582: 
9583: IOC_W_MASK           Window
9584:         <31:20>         Size
9585: 
9586: 0000 0000 0000          1 MB
9587: 0000 0000 0001          2 MB
9588: 0000 0000 0011          4 MB
9589: 0000 0000 0111          8 MB
9590: 0000 0000 1111        16 MB
9591: 0000 0001 1111        32 MB
9592: 0000 0011 1111        64 MB
9593: 0000 0111 1111       128 MB
9594: 0000 1111 1111       256 MB
9595: 0001 1111 1111       512 MB
9596: 0011 1111 1111          1 GB
9597: 0111 1111 1111          2 GB
9598: 1111 1111 1111          4 GB
9599: 
9600: IOC_W_BASE register bits <31:20> specify the target window starting address
9601: in the PCI memory address space. The IOC_W_BASE and IOC_W_MASK
9602: registers determine when a PCI transaction references an address in a target
9603: window, as follows:
9604: 
9605: 1. Each of the incoming PCI address bits <31:20> is exclusive-ORed with
9606:      the corresponding bit of each window base register (IOC_W_BASE1,
9607:      IOC_W_BASE0, bits <31:20>).
9608: 
9609: 2. The result of each exclusive-OR is then ANDed with the complement
9610:      of the corresponding bits of the corresponding window mask register
9611:      (IOC_W_MASK1, IOC_W_MASK0, bits <31:20>).
9612: 
9613: 3. The result of the AND operation for each window is then ORed to produce
9614:      a hit (match) or miss indication. If the result of the final OR is a zero for
9615:      a given window, the incoming address has hit in that PCI target window;
9616:      otherwise, it has missed in that window.
9617: 
9618:                                                     Note
9619: 
9620:      The window base address must be on a naturally aligned address
9621:      boundary equal to the window size (Table 6-6). Additionally, to ensure
9622:      that only one PCI target window can match an incoming PCI address,
9623:      the PCI target windows must be programmed so that they do not
9624:      respond to overlapping PCI address ranges.
9625: 
9626: 6-24 I/O Controller
9627:             Each target window is independently enabled when the window enable bit
9628:             (WEN) is set in the corresponding IOC_W_BASE register. The IOC responds
9629:             only to PCI-initiated transfers that hit in an enabled window. If the WEN bit
9630:             is cleared, the IOC ignores PCI-initiated transfers that hit in that window.
9631:             When a hit occurs in an enabled PCI target window, the IOC responds to
9632:             the PCI cycle by asserting the devsel_l signal. The IOC translates the
9633:             32-bit PCI address to a 34-bit CPU address. The scatter-gather (SG) bit
9634:             in the corresponding IOC_W_BASE register determines how the address
9635:             is translated. If the SG bit is clear, direct-mapped address translation is
9636:             used (Section 6.3.2.1). If the SG bit is set, scatter-gather mapped address
9637:             translation is used (Section 6.3.2.2).
9638: 6.3.2.1 Direct-Mapped Address Translation
9639:             Direct-mapped address translation is used when an incoming PCI address
9640:             hits in a PCI target window and the SG bit is not set in the corresponding
9641:             IOC_W_BASE register. The translated address is generated by concatenating
9642:             bits from the corresponding IOC_T_BASE register with bits from the incoming
9643:             PCI address. Bits <33:32> of the translated address are always zero. The
9644:             IOC_W_MASK register determines which IOC_T_BASE register bits and PCI
9645:             address bits are concatenated to form the translated address, as shown in
9646:             Table 6-7. The translated address is the starting address in system memory
9647:             for the PCI transaction.
9648: 
9649:                                                                Caution
902:        � Appendix D, Technical Support and Ordering Information, describes how
903:             to obtain information and technical support, and order products and
904:             associated literature.
