2495: 2.10 Physical Address Space
2496: 
2497:             Internally, the chip uses a 34-bit address space divided into regions as shown
2498:             in Table 2-6. Addresses in the cacheable memory region update the optional
2499:             Bcache when referenced by the CPU. The graphics memory region supports
2500:             simple graphics operations on data. In the PCI sparse memory region,
2501:             addresses are shifted 5 bits to allow access size encodings. Addresses are not
2502:             shifted in the PCI dense memory region. PCI peripherals can access only the
2503:             cacheable and noncacheable memory regions because the IOC maps the 32-bit
2504:             PCI address to the internal address that can access only those memory spaces.
2505:             In other words, the PCI cannot access the IOC register, memory controller
2506:             register, or graphics memory regions. (The translation mechanism is described
2507:             in Chapter 6.)
2508: 
2509:             Instructions cannot be executed from the upper three quadrants of memory;
2510:             that is, when address bits <33:32> = 01, 10, or 11. Instructions can be executed
2511:             from the cacheable and noncacheable regions of the lowest quadrant, in which
2512:             case they will be cached in the Icache but not the Bcache.
