1571:             When the processor receives an interrupt request and that request is enabled
1572:             and the processor is not currently executing PALcode, the hardware reports or
1573:             delivers an interrupt to the exception logic. Before vectoring to the interrupt-
1574:             service PAL dispatch address, the pipeline is completely drained and all
1575:             outstanding Dcache fills are completed. The restart address is saved in the
1576:             exception address register (EXC_ADDR, Section 4.1.8) and the processor enters
1577:             PALmode. The cause of the interrupt can be determined by examining the
1578:             state of the interrupt request registers.
1579: 
1580:             Usually, the interrupts from the irq<2:0> pins can be grouped as follows:
1581: 
1582:             � Interval timer interrupts
1583: 
1584:             � Halt, powerfail, ISA and EISA nonmaskable interrupts, and the PCI serr_l
1585:                   signal
1586: 
1587:             � ISA and EISA interrupt requests, and PCI interrupt requests
1588: 
1589:             Hardware interrupt requests are level-sensitive; therefore, they can be removed
1590:             before an interrupt is serviced. If they are removed before the interrupt
1591:             request register is read, the register will return a zero value.
